course: dictionary
lesson: 15018
title: Respective and respectively
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Respective is a formal word. It means belonging to each one of the people or things just named. It usually comes before a noun, like «their respective homes».
explain: «They went to their respective homes.» Each person went to his or her own home. Respective points to each one separately, so you can think of it as "each one's own".
explain: «Tom and Ann are 8 and 10 respectively.» Respectively means "in the order just given". The first name goes with the first number, so Tom is 8 and Ann is 10. You will often meet this word in maths problems and exam questions.
fact: Respective is an adjective, and it is used before a noun, like «their respective jobs».
fact: Respectively is an adverb, and it means «in the order just given».
fact: «Respective» means each one's own, when several people or things are named.
fact: When you see «respectively», match the first item with the first, the second with the second, and so on.
fact: Useful phrases: «their respective» and «respectively».
exercise: «Sam and Priya went to their respective classrooms.» What does respective mean here? | *Each one's own | Very fast | Empty and quiet | Together in one room
exercise: «Tom and Ann are 8 and 10 respectively.» How old is Ann? | *10 | 18 | 2 | 8
exercise: Which word means "in the order just given"? | Both | Together | Respective | *Respectively
test: «The twins hung up their respective coats.» Which word does respective come before? | Hung | Their | Twins | *Coats
test: «Mia, Leo and Jo scored 5, 7 and 9 respectively.» Who scored 7? | Mia | Nobody | *Leo | Jo
exam: «Lily, Omar and Ben finished first, second and third respectively.» Who finished second? | *Omar | Ben | Lily | Nobody
